{"name":"Siliceous Red","hex":"#5a3d4a","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"siliceous","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of, relating to, consisting of, or resembling silica or silicates.","Comprised of biogenic silicate minerals. Contrasted with the inorganic term siliciclastic."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/siliceous"},{"term":"red","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of the setting sun, blood, and strawberries; the color which is evoked by the longest visible wavelengths (between about 625–740 nm), and a primary additive color.","A revolutionary socialist or (most commonly) a Communist;  a Bolshevik, a supporter of the Bolsheviks in the Russian Civil War."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a red hue.","Having an orange-brown or orange-blond colour; ginger."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/red"}],"entries":[{"title":"Silicon dioxide","blocks":[{"text":"Silicon dioxide, also known as silica, is an oxide of silicon with the chemical formula SiO2, and is commonly found in nature as quartz. In many parts of the world, silica is the major constituent of sand. Silica is one of the most complex and abundant families of materials, existing as a compound of several minerals and as a synthetic product. Examples include fused quartz, fumed silica, opal, and aerogels. It is used in structural materials, microelectronics, and as components in the food and pharmaceutical industries. All forms are white or colorless, although impure samples can be colored or opaque."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Sample_of_silicon_dioxide.jpg/330px-Sample_of_silicon_dioxide.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silicon_dioxide"},{"title":"Red","blocks":[{"text":"Red is the color at the long wavelength end of the visible spectrum of light, next to orange and opposite violet. It has a dominant wavelength of approximately 625–750 nanometers. It is a primary color in the RGB color model and a secondary color in the CMYK color model, and is the complementary color of cyan. Reds range from the brilliant yellow-tinged scarlet and vermillion to bluish-red crimson, and vary in shade from the pale red pink to the dark red burgundy."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Delicious_fresh_strawberries_%284735910403%29.jpg/330px-Delicious_fresh_strawberries_%284735910403%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/SiO2repeat.png/500px-SiO2repeat.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/SiO2repeat.png/500px-SiO2repeat.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Structural motif found in α-quartz, but also found in almost all forms of silicon dioxide","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASiO2repeat.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Quartzrn.PNG/500px-Quartzrn.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Quartzrn.PNG/1280px-Quartzrn.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Quartzrn.PNG/500px-Quartzrn.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Relationship between refractive index and density for some SiO2 forms[7]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AQuartzrn.PNG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Kieselsaeure380m2prog.jpg/500px-Kieselsaeure380m2prog.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","sr